WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.224
Hi, and welcome to CSC-239, Java
Programming, taught by Professor

2
00:00:05.224 --> 00:00:08.634
Harris Williams at Bunker Hill Community
College.

3
00:00:08.914 --> 00:00:12.487
You've written code before, and now you'll
build real Java programs.

4
00:00:13.087 --> 00:00:16.634
This course is fully online and
asynchronous, so you'll work on

5
00:00:16.634 --> 00:00:19.335
your own schedule, with a new module every
week.

6
00:00:19.635 --> 00:00:21.672
Here's what you'll be able to do by the end.

7
00:00:21.923 --> 00:00:25.345
You'll design Java programs using control
flow, methods,

8
00:00:25.345 --> 00:00:27.790
collections, and object-oriented design.

9
00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:31.510
You'll test your programs, handle errors,
and work with file data.

10
00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:36.235
You'll build graphical interfaces and
coordinate tasks that run at the same time.

11
00:00:36.455 --> 00:00:39.664
And you'll package it all into working Java
word-game projects.

12
00:00:40.264 --> 00:00:42.397
So how do we get there? Module by module.

13
00:00:42.697 --> 00:00:46.163
We start with Variable Assignment: Java
types, strings,

14
00:00:46.163 --> 00:00:48.999
calculations, and input that produce
results.

15
00:00:49.199 --> 00:00:52.663
Then Flow Control, where conditions and
loops steer your program.

16
00:00:52.864 --> 00:00:55.804
In Static Functions you'll write reusable
methods

17
00:00:55.804 --> 00:00:58.204
with typed parameters and return values.

18
00:00:58.404 --> 00:01:03.268
And in Data Structures you'll choose arrays,
lists, sets, and maps to organize your data.

19
00:01:03.618 --> 00:01:07.538
Then the design work begins. Classes and
Objects has you design

20
00:01:07.538 --> 00:01:10.898
classes that protect data and provide useful
behavior.

21
00:01:11.098 --> 00:01:13.919
Abstraction and Interfaces adds interfaces,

22
00:01:13.919 --> 00:01:17.001
inheritance, and generics for flexible
designs.

23
00:01:17.201 --> 00:01:20.415
In Exceptions and Testing you'll handle
failures and

24
00:01:20.415 --> 00:01:22.702
confirm expected behavior with JUnit.

25
00:01:22.902 --> 00:01:27.714
Text File I/O has you read and write text
files and search dictionary prefixes.

26
00:01:27.964 --> 00:01:31.697
That's everything you need for the Ghost
Project: build, test,

27
00:01:31.697 --> 00:01:34.587
and package a dictionary-based word-game
player.

28
00:01:34.937 --> 00:01:37.522
As your skills grow, Functional Programming
and

29
00:01:37.522 --> 00:01:39.942
Streams brings lambdas and stream pipelines.

30
00:01:40.142 --> 00:01:44.248
Binary File I/O saves and restores typed
binary data and objects.

31
00:01:44.448 --> 00:01:49.221
JavaFX brings layouts, events, and
validation to a graphical interface.

32
00:01:49.422 --> 00:01:52.593
And Multithreading coordinates concurrent
tasks with

33
00:01:52.593 --> 00:01:54.970
threads and producer-consumer patterns.

34
00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:58.125
Finally, the Super Ghost Project brings it
all

35
00:01:58.125 --> 00:02:00.714
together in one tested, packaged program.

36
00:02:01.314 --> 00:02:03.323
So what does a typical module look like?

37
00:02:03.603 --> 00:02:06.996
Read and watch the Learning Materials, run
the examples and

38
00:02:06.996 --> 00:02:10.101
complete the practice, then complete the
Deliverables.

39
00:02:10.351 --> 00:02:13.262
Most modules include a discussion, and
assignments

40
00:02:13.262 --> 00:02:15.242
are not required for every module.

41
00:02:15.493 --> 00:02:18.855
When a coding assignment is there, you can
resubmit it as

42
00:02:18.855 --> 00:02:21.391
many times as you need through December 18.

43
00:02:21.691 --> 00:02:25.805
Two milestones anchor the course: the Ghost
Project in Module 09,

44
00:02:25.805 --> 00:02:28.779
and the Super Ghost Final Project in Module
14.

45
00:02:29.029 --> 00:02:32.353
You'll submit each one in Canvas as a
runnable JAR, or a

46
00:02:32.353 --> 00:02:34.787
ZIP containing the JAR and support files.

47
00:02:35.387 --> 00:02:37.314
Now let's look at where that work happens.

48
00:02:37.564 --> 00:02:41.207
Everything starts in Canvas. In the course
navigation on the

49
00:02:41.207 --> 00:02:44.061
left, click Modules to see the course in
order.

50
00:02:44.411 --> 00:02:47.327
Inside each module, the Learning Materials
come

51
00:02:47.327 --> 00:02:49.933
first: the readings, videos, and examples.

52
00:02:50.153 --> 00:02:54.399
The Deliverables follow, so you always know
what to study and what to turn in.

53
00:02:54.649 --> 00:02:58.574
There's no textbook to buy; course content
lives in Jupyter notebooks.

54
00:02:58.924 --> 00:03:02.679
To open them, click Grader Than in that same
left-hand navigation.

55
00:03:02.899 --> 00:03:06.381
That opens your Workspace right in the
browser, already set

56
00:03:06.381 --> 00:03:08.918
up for Java, so there's nothing to install.

57
00:03:09.168 --> 00:03:12.131
Coding assignments are designed to run
through the

58
00:03:12.131 --> 00:03:15.390
autograder, which checks your work and
returns feedback

59
00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:18.235
quickly, so you can fix and resubmit right
away.

60
00:03:18.835 --> 00:03:20.954
A few things make this course go smoothly.

61
00:03:21.204 --> 00:03:24.247
Start each module early, run every example
yourself,

62
00:03:24.247 --> 00:03:26.413
and ask questions when you get stuck.

63
00:03:26.663 --> 00:03:30.006
The Question Board is always open. It's
ungraded, there's

64
00:03:30.006 --> 00:03:32.469
no minimum, and I'd love to see you there.

65
00:03:32.719 --> 00:03:36.132
You can also email me, or schedule office
hours using the

66
00:03:36.132 --> 00:03:38.466
link on the course home page in Canvas.

67
00:03:39.016 --> 00:03:41.133
I am looking forward to seeing what you
build.

68
00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:43.006
Let's get started with Module 01.
